text:
Ah, well met, young apprentice! You've come seeking a spell, but before I give it to you, you must understand a truth a thousand wizards have learned the hard way: eloquence is not magic — it is craft, patience, and practice honed over a lifetime. Still, a spell to aid you I shall offer.

This is not the \*The Tongue of Fire and Moon\*, nor any charm from ancient grimoirs. This is a spell born of old knowledge, passed from bard to bard for centuries, sometimes called **the Seven Stances of Mira**, a court minstrel from the Age of Sigils who became known as the single most persuasive speaker in all the Seven Kingdoms. Her name lives on in her own stanza:

*"Speak knowingly, not memorized;  
Speak truly, not always aligned;  
Listen twice what you would say;  
Take the pause when words decay;  
Know one truth that stands apart —  
A single story, well unspooled,  
Can break a spell no charm could surmount."*

So here is your spell: **Speak knowingly, not memorized.** It is a mantra, a charm whispered beneath your tongue, to anchor your voice in real thought rather than practiced rhythm. Speak truly, not always aligned — give the truth its proper due, even when it does not serve your case. Listen twice what you would say — hearing the other's words clearly before answering is the best preamble to any trade of rhetoric. Take the pause when words decay — hesitation for a breath, out of rhythm and into clarity, is often the spell a room forgets plentiful. And know one truth that stands apart — a single story, once unspooled, can break the spell of any well-worn argument no charm could manage.

Lest you think this a trick: the deepest eloquence is no spell at all, but knowing when to speak, knowing what matters, and knowing when silence is more eloquent than any word. Keep those seven stances close to you, and your tongue will always carry its weight in the halls of any court.
